\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Trace record and state boundary}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}
\tag{state}

\p{A long-horizon training trace should preserve the behavior that a later
agent must learn: its decisions, evidence, verification, recovery, and the
state boundary visible to the continuation. Fix event domains
#{\mathcal O_{\rm evt}}, #{\mathcal A_{\rm evt}}, #{\mathcal Y_{\rm evt}},
#{\mathcal E_{\rm evt}}, and #{\mathcal V_{\rm evt}}, together with a
termination domain #{\mathcal T_{\rm evt}} and a boundary domain
#{\mathcal S_{\rm boundary}}.}

\p{For #{H\in\mathbb N}, a \newvocab{trace record} is}
##{
 T=(o_{0:H},a_{0:H},y_{0:H},e_{0:H},v_{0:H},\tau,\sigma,\kappa)
 \in\mathcal O_{\rm evt}^{H+1}\times\mathcal A_{\rm evt}^{H+1}
 \times\mathcal Y_{\rm evt}^{H+1}\times\mathcal E_{\rm evt}^{H+1}
 \times\mathcal V_{\rm evt}^{H+1}\times\mathcal T_{\rm evt}
 \times\mathcal S_{\rm boundary}\times\mathbb N.
}
\p{The components #{o}, #{a}, and #{y} are observation, action, and outcome
events; #{e} records evidence or an environment-state reference; #{v} records
verification or recovery; #{\tau} records termination; #{\sigma} records the
state boundary; and #{\kappa} is token length. The horizon #{H} and token
length #{\kappa} are separate coordinates. A task may use a declared subtype
of each event domain, but it must preserve the coordinate meaning.}

\p{The record is a training datum only together with its declared boundary
#{\sigma}: a continuation may access exactly the state coordinates allowed by
that boundary. The record therefore supports a later selector or run kernel
without treating token count as a proxy for the events that make continuation
useful.}

\p{This is a protocol definition rather than a formal theorem; the event
domains and transition semantics must be instantiated by the experiment that
uses the record.}
